        "title": "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Given that $F=2x{y}^{2}\\,i+4{x}^{2}y\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\u2207\\cdot F=\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot F & =\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(2x{y}^{2})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(4{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/key": "selection",
            ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
            ":answer-field/choices": [
              {
                ":answer/id": "aa1f9d4c-f8d5-57d4-bf61-02845bbec994",
