      },
      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Consider the vector field $F=({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y)\\,i-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\u2207\\cdot F$ at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\u2207\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot F & =\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y)+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y-{e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y). \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point  $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\u2207\\cdot F)(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
