        "title": "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Consider the vector field $F=x{e}^{y}\\,i+xy{e}^{y}\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\u2207\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,0).$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\u2207\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot F & =\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x{e}^{y})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(xy{e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y}+x{e}^{y}+xy{e}^{y}. \\\\  & =(1+x+xy){e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\u2207\\cdot F)(2,0) & =(1+2+2(0)){e}^{0} \\\\  & =3{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
