      },
      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Consider the vector field $F=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\u2207\\cdot F$ at the point $(3,4).$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\u2207\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot F & =\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(3,4),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\u2207\\cdot F)(3,4) & =3\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
