      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Consider the vector field $F={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1).$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\u2207\\cdot F=\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot F & =\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x))+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)+\\frac{x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\u2207\\cdot F)(\\pi ,1) & =2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi )+\\frac{\\pi }{1} \\\\  & =2(1)(1)+\\pi  \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
